    commentary: `Source structure, exactly as printed (Guptavati-adi sapta-tika, 814-pp PDF). Place in the edition: mula 1.3 at PDF p.131; tikas at PDF pp.131-132. (1) Mula verbatim, preserved line-for-line above: svarocishe'ntare purvam caitra-vamsha-samudbhavah / suratho nama rajabhut samaste kshit mandale — rendering as in translation below. (2) (1) Guptavati, PDF p.131: marker only (iti 3), no gloss on this verse. (3) (2) Chaturdhari verbatim in substance, PDF p.131: atretihasam avatarayati; svarocisho'patyam svarocisha iti svarocisho nama dvitiyo manuh, tad-adhikaropalakshite svarocishe'ntare samaye (dvitiya-manvantare); manvantaram manoh kalah (vishnupurana-darshanat); purvam katha-kalapekshaya; caitro nama svarocishasya manos sunuh tasya vamshe santatau samudbhavo janma yasya; nameti prasiddhau, suratha-samnjaya prasiddha ityarthah — rendering: here the Itihasa descends; in the age of Svarocisha, the second Manu; long before our tale, in Caitra's line (Caitra, that Manu's son), the famed one called Suratha. (4) (3) Shantanavi verbatim in substance, pp.131-132: ashtamo manuh kathyata iti prag yah prastutas tasya savarnah prag-janana-nidana-tamahi-macaritrani varnayati markandeyo'nena shlokena; purvam prathamam svayambhuva-manvantaram apekshya dvitiya-manvantara-lakshane svarocishe'ntare; svasya-atmana iva brahmana iva rocih prabha yasya sah svarocir manuh (athava ah vishnuh sushthu asyeva vishnor iva rocish chavir yasya sah); svarocisha idam svarocisham; antara-avasanam rati grihnaty antaram tasmin kala-visheshe; citi samjnane (sarva-dhatubhyas tran, cetati janati dharmadharma-pravritty-apravritti-kartum iti citrah kashcid raja, tasyapatyam puman caitrah, shivadibhyo'n); tasya vamshah santanah tasmat samudbhavati iti caitra-vamsha-samudbhavah (vidyaya janmana va praninam eka-lakshanah santano vamsha ityucyate); ramante'smin rathah, shobhano ratho yasya sah surathah; rajate iti raja; abhut (bhu sattayam lun); kshiter bhumer mandalam cakravalam tasmin — rendering: with this verse Markandeya tells the former births and glories of Savarna's line; Svarocisha (self-luminous as Brahman, or beauteous as Vishnu), second age after Swayambhuva; Caitra the knower-king, his line, Suratha of fair chariot, who shone as king over earth's whole disc. (5) (4) Nagojibhatti verbatim, PDF p.132: svarocishadhikaropalakshite dvitiya-manvantare; purvam iti katha-kalapekshaya; caitras tasyaiva manos sutah; nameti prasiddhau — rendering: the second Manvantara under Svarocisha; before, relative to tale-time; Caitra, that very Manu's son; famed by name. (6) (5) Jagaccandrachandrika, PDF p.132: marker only (svarocisha iti 4, iti 3). (7) (6) Damshoddhara verbatim, PDF p.132: svarocisha iti | svarocishe dvitiya-manvantare | caitrah svarocishasya sutah — rendering as in (5) above. (8) (7) Chandraprabha Hindi verbatim, PDF p.132: bahut pahle svarocisha manvantara (dvitiya manu) mem caitravamsha mem utpanna suratha namak ek raja the, jinka sampurna kshiti-mandala para adhikara tha — rendering: long ago, in the Svarocisha age, a king named Suratha born in Caitra's line ruled the whole earth-disc. Diacritics are given here in simplified ASCII to keep the source record robust; devanagari above preserves the print aksharas exactly, and the Sadhana-Samara plus Seven-Digest reading follows unaltered. **Sādhana-Samara Commentary (Hindi source: Svārociṣe’ntare):**
1. **Not mere fable (rūpaka-mātra) but true event in you**: What was enacted in the outer kingdom is enacted identically in the consciousness-kingdom (caitanya-kṣetra) of every jīva. As “whatever is in the cosmos is in this body” (yad brahmāṇḍe tad piṇḍe), no liberated seeker exists in whose heart the Kurukṣetra of the Gītā or the Devāsura-war of Caṇḍī has not unfolded. Some notice it while walking; others look back after reaching and see they came through Gītā and Caṇḍī unknowingly. We enter Caṇḍī chiefly for its ādhyātmika (inner-yogic) and ādhidaivika (divine-energy) secret — “O Ma, open our eye of wisdom.”
2. **Svārociṣa = heavenly light**: sva (own/divine) + rocis (radiance). When the inner land (antara-deśa) is lit by divine light, the jīva becomes Suratha. The method: learn to receive every object as affectionate Mahāmāyā, chase every bhāva crying “Ma, Ma” with a child’s simple heart, keep bhāva-mayī nāma-rūpa-mayī Mahāmāyā in the heart — then the true Mother-awareness wakes. This verse is the seed (bīja) of the whole narrative section: how, having gained spiritual strength (kṣetra + bala), the Caṇḍī-principle announces itself in the heart.
3. **Who is Suratha? (for beginners)**: su (good, well-guided) + ratha (chariot = body-mind vehicle). He is your own ruling intellect and will — ethical, capable, sovereign of “the whole earth” (your entire life-field) — yet about to be betrayed and exiled. Only an exiled, wounded king sincerely asks “why do the wise still suffer delusion?” Thus worldly fall becomes the doorway to Sage Medhas (prajñā).

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
Here the narrative proper begins with King Suratha, and the commentators focus on establishing him as a legitimately great but ultimately vulnerable ruler - necessary groundwork for the crisis to come. Pradipa notes the specific dynastic details (the Caitra lineage, the era of Svarocisha Manu) are not incidental ornament but anchor the story within the larger Puranic chronology the audience is assumed to know, lending the episode historical, not merely allegorical, weight in the tradition's own self-understanding. Nagojibhatti and Damsoddhara, true to their grammar-first approach, spend their notes resolving the compound 'sakalaksitipah' (sovereign of the entire earth) and confirming Suratha's universal, not merely regional, kingship - which matters later, since his loss will be total, not partial. The Hindi Candraprabha keeps to simple narrative restatement, letting the king's introduction read as it would in any Puranic tale.`,
    keyPoints: ["Two pādas ending in Devanagari ३, no speaker line, PDF p.131 lineation preserved", "Source order in print: mūla p.131, seven tikas pp.131-132 (Guptavati and Jagaccandra markers only)", "**Svārociṣa = Inner Illumination**: When the heart-land is lit by divine light, the aspirant becomes fit for the Caṇḍī-battle.","**Story is Inner History**: The palace war outside is simultaneously happening inside you; Gita and Candi must both ripen in every seeker.","**Suratha = Well-Charioted Intellect**: Your own ethical will, lord of your life-field, whose betrayal births true spiritual questioning."]
