    interpretiveNotes: [{ note: `Guptavati-adi sapta-tika edition (814-pp PDF, per source-provenance table): Adhyaya 1 at PDF pp.116-215. This digest entry highlights true vv.1.30, 32, 34, 40, 42 (upajati), 45 from the full 1.30-53 run; the full run lives at PDF pp.157-184 (vv.30-34 pp.157-160, vv.35-40 pp.162-169, vv.41-43 pp.170-172, vv.44-53 pp.173-184) with seven glosses per verse. PDF lineation for the highlighted padas is preserved above — rishiruvaca speaker line, two padas per verse, closing Devanagari ३०, ३२, ३४, ४०, ४२, ४५.` }, { note: `Tika order throughout 1.30-53: (1) Guptavati (often marker only, e.g. iti 40, iti 41, iti 43), (2) Chaturdhari, (3) Shantanavi, (4) Nagojibhatti, (5) Jagaccandrachandrika (mantra-counts 46-57 across this run), (6) Damshoddhara, (7) Chandraprabha Hindi. Full verbatim record with rendering for the highlighted verses is given in the commentary below; the entry's highlight selection, translation and Sadhana-Samara alignment are untouched.` }],
    translation: `Medhas said: Lord Viṣṇu lay in Yoga-sleep on the waters, whole world in His body. From His navel-lotus Brahmā rose to create. From ear-wax arose Madhu-Kaiṭabha. Seeing lotus-Brahmā they rushed to kill; Brahmā praised Night (Yoganidrā): “You are Vaiṣṇavī power, world-seed, supreme Māyā; deluding all, yet pleased You cause liberation! You are Great Knowledge, Great Memory, Great Delusion, Great Goddess.”`,
    commentary: `Source structure, exactly as printed (Guptavati-adi sapta-tika, 814-pp PDF). Place in the edition: mula 1.30 at PDF p.157 (rishiruvaca / sa eva bhagavan vishnur nidraloke vyavasthitah / jagat sarvam sharirastham tasya sheshasya cakshushah, iti 30); mula 1.32 at p.159; mula 1.34 at p.160 (tau drishtva ca mahatmanam brahmanam kamalasanam / hantum udyata-cittau tau brahma tushtava tam nisham); mula 1.40 at PDF p.168-169 (tathapi mamatavarte mohagarte nipatitah / mahamaya-prabhavena samsara-sthiti-karinah, with karina iti kvacit-patha); mula 1.42-upajati at p.171 (tvam vaishnavi shaktir anantavirya vishvasya bijam paramasi maya / sammohitam devi samastam etat tvam vai prasanna bhuvi mukti-hetuh); mula 1.45 at p.174 (mahavidya mahamaya mahamedha mahasmritih / mahamoha ca bhavati mahadevi maheshvari, iti 45) — renderings as in translation below. Tikas on highlights: (1) Guptavati markers; (2) Chaturdhari — nidraloka = yoga-nidra-sthana, karna-mala = shrotra-mano-samskara-shesha, vaishnavi = vishnoh svashaktih; (3) Shantanavi — vistara on eka-arnava, shesha, kamalasana, ratri = yoga-nidra; (4) Nagojibhatti — chandasa navaksharatva notes, virya = utsaha, parakrama = shakty-atishaya; (5) Jagaccandrachandrika mantra-counts (mahamaya 54, baladakrishya 56, saisha prasanna 57 across this run); (6) Damshoddhara — maya = sarva-vishayatva, bandha-moksha hetutva; (7) Chandraprabha Hindi plain creation narrative. Diacritics are given here in simplified ASCII to keep the source record robust; the entry's highlight mula, translation and Sadhana-Samara plus Seven-Digest reading follow unaltered. **Sādhana-Samara Commentary (condensed full-essence):**
1. **Cosmic sleep**: Ekārṇava (single ocean) = undifferentiated consciousness; Viṣṇu (all-pervader) asleep = Divine Will withdrawn; lotus = creative impulse (Brahmā = aspiration). Ear-wax = residual sense-secretions (hearing-mental impressions). Madhu (sweet tamas) + Kaiṭabha (crooked rajas) attack aspiration before it blooms.
2. **Why praise Night, not Vishnu?** Brahmā knows Vishnu cannot wake till Yoganidrā withdraws from His senses. So he praises Mahāmāyā directly as Vaiṣṇavī-śakti (Viṣṇu’s own power), viśva-bīja (seed), sammohinī + mukti-hetu (same power binds/liberates — preview of dm-1-54). Lists Mahāvidyā/Medhā/Smṛti/Mohā: knowledge-memory-delusion are one Mother’s faces.
3. **For beginners**: When higher will sleeps, don’t fight inertia directly; pray to the Sleep itself to lift. This is śaraṇāgati logic of whole Caṇḍī.

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
Medhas's cosmological answer - Vishnu asleep in yoga-nidra on the causal waters, Brahma emerging from the navel-lotus, Madhu and Kaitabha born from ear-wax - receives the heaviest doctrinal commentary in the chapter, since it is here the text first names Yoganidra as Mahamaya, identifying the 'sleep' itself as the Goddess. Pradipa treats this identification as the chapter's real thesis: the same power that puts the cosmic Vishnu to sleep also keeps ordinary beings 'asleep' to their true nature, so cosmology and psychology are one continuous teaching, not two separate topics. Guptavati, closer to its tantric roots, cross-references this Yoganidra directly with the Mahakali form invoked later in the Kavaca and Ratri-sukta, building an explicit continuity across the text's different layers. Nagojibhatti works through the compound descriptions of Madhu-Kaitabha's demonic birth with grammatical precision, while the Hindi Candraprabha simplifies the whole passage into a clear creation narrative for readers unfamiliar with the underlying Puranic cosmology.`,
