    interpretiveNotes: [{ note: `Guptavati-adi sapta-tika edition (814-pp PDF, per source-provenance table): Adhyaya 1 at PDF pp.116-215. Mula of true v.1.33 at PDF pp.159-160 (tat kim etan mahabhaga yan moho jnaninor api / mamasya ca bhavaty esha vivekandhasya mudhata, iti 33) with its seven glosses at pp.159-160. PDF lineation preserved above — no speaker line (Suratha's question continues), two padas, closing ॥३३॥ in print.` }, { note: `Tika order on 1.33: (1) Guptavati (vivekandhasya = viveka-cakshusha hinasya va; jnana-ajnanayoh samanadhikaranyam katham iti prashnarthah), (2) Chaturdhari (tatkeneetat-patha fn.), (3) Shantanavi, (4) Nagojibhatti, (5) Jagaccandrachandrika (mantra-count 45: mamasya ca bhavaty esha vivekandhasya mudhata svaha), (6) Damshoddhara, (7) Chandraprabha Hindi. Verbatim record with rendering is given in the commentary below.` }],
    translation: `Suratha said: O most fortunate one, what is this — that delusion grips even the two of us, who understand it as delusion? This blindness to our own discrimination — mine and this man's — what could it possibly be?`,
    commentary: `Source structure, exactly as printed (Guptavati-adi sapta-tika, 814-pp PDF). Place in the edition: mula 1.33 at PDF pp.159-160 closing with 33; tikas pp.159-160. Mula verbatim, preserved above: tat kim etan mahabhaga yan moho jnaninor api / mamasya ca bhavaty esha vivekandhasya mudhata — rendering as in translation below (what is this delusion even in us two knowers, this blindness-in-discernment?). Tika digest: (1) Guptavati — andha = acakshushi tamasy andham, jala-timira examples; (2) Chaturdhari — jnaninor api = vishaye doshavattva-jnanavator api; (3) Shantanavi — he mahabhaga he sumedho mune, nripa-vaishyayor moho bhavatiti; (4) Nagojibhatti — ubhaya-nishtha-mohasya ayuktatvam; (5) Jagaccandra mantra 45; (6) Damshoddhara — bhagasya aishvary-ader idam bhagam; (7) Chandraprabha Hindi — mere aur inke jaise ham donon jnaniyon mem bhi jo moha. Diacritics are given here in simplified ASCII to keep the source record robust; devanagari above preserves the print aksharas exactly, and the Sadhana-Samara plus Seven-Digest reading follows unaltered. **Sādhana-Samara Commentary:**
Suratha's question sharpens here into its most precise form: not "why do people grieve" in the abstract, but "why do *we*, who can name our own confusion as confusion, remain caught inside it anyway." This is the exact gap between intellectual and embodied knowledge that Medhas's coming teaching on Mahāmāyā will address — the text refuses to let the aspirant believe that correct information alone dissolves attachment.

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
Guptavati's brief note glosses "vivekandhasya" (of one blinded despite discrimination) as the verse's key compound, underscoring that the paradox — knowing and still not-escaping — is being named explicitly rather than left implicit. The commentators broadly agree this verse functions as the direct pivot into Medhas's forthcoming discourse on Mahamaya: everything Suratha and Samadhi have observed about themselves and, in the following verses, about animal behavior, converges on the single question this verse poses.`,
