    translation: `Sage Medhas replied: "She, the blessed Goddess Mahāmāyā, forcibly draws even the minds of the wise and casts them into delusion! By Her this entire moving and unmoving universe is projected. And She alone, when propitiated with devotion, becomes the bestower of boons and grants supreme liberation!"`,
    commentary: `Source structure, exactly as printed (Guptavati-adi sapta-tika, 814-pp PDF). Recension note: the entry mula above follows the vulgate mantra-ganana (numbered 1.54-55 in some editions); the Guptavati print at true vv.1.42-43 (PDF pp.171-172) reads jnaninam api cetamsi devi bhagavati hi sa / balad akrishya mohaya mahamaya prayacchati (iti 42) and taya visrijyate vishvam trailokyam sacaracaram / saisha prasanna varada nrinam bhavati muktaye (iti 43, with jagad etac caracaram kvacit-patha). Both numberings denote one passage: the balad-akrishya aphorism plus the prasanna-mukti seal. Print mula verbatim: jnaninam api ... prayacchati; taya visrijyate ... muktaye — renderings as in translation below. Tika digest on true vv.42-43: (1) Guptavati — anyesham kim uteti tatparyam; (2) Chaturdhari — balat sva-shaktitah akrishya vivekavyavartya; (3) Shantanavi — upanishat-uttha-jnaninam api pumsam cetamsi; (4) Nagoji — sva-kartavya-srishti-vriddhaye evam-prabhava sa; (5) Jagaccandra 56-57; (6) Damshoddhara — ka Mutika nyayena; (7) Chandraprabha Hindi — kyonki vahi devi bhagavati mahamaya jnaniyon ke bhi chitton ko balapurvak khinchkar moha ko de deti hai; usi ke dvara yah vishva utpanna. Diacritics are given here in simplified ASCII to keep the source record robust; the entry's vulgate mula, translation and Sadhana-Samara plus Seven-Digest reading follow unaltered. **Sādhana-Samara Commentary:**
This is the foundational aphorism of the Śākta worldview. *Mahāmāyā* is not an accidental blemish, nor is She inert illusion (*Mithyā* in the sense of an absolute void). She is *Bhagavatī*—the conscious sovereign power (*Cit-Śakti*) of the Supreme Divine. 
Her power operates in two modes:
1. *Avidyā-Rūpa* (The Veiling Power): Forcibly drawing even contemplative minds outward into the cosmic drama (*Balād-ākṛṣya*).
2. *Vidyā-Rūpa* (The Unveiling Grace): When worshipped not as external world-matter but as the Mother-Consciousness, She turns Her gaze (*Prasannā*) and dissolves all bondages, granting *Mukti*. 
Hence, the spiritual aspirant must not combat Nature as an enemy, but propitiate Her as the affectionate Divine Mother who holds the keys to liberation.

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
This verse describes Mahamaya's power to overwhelm even the minds of the wise, and Pradipa uses it to address a natural objection: if even sages and gods can be deluded, what use is spiritual effort at all? Its answer is that delusion here is not permanent defeat but a recurring condition that makes grace, not self-effort alone, necessary - precisely the argument that justifies the entire Saptashati as a text of supplication rather than pure philosophy. Caturdhari and Santanavi note the verse's placement immediately before Brahma's request that the gods together approach the sleeping Vishnu, reading verses 42-55 as a single connected unit rather than isolated statements. The editorial note in this edition on the verse-numbering discrepancy (some vulgate texts count these as 1.42-43, others as 1.54-55) is itself flagged by the editor as a manuscript-tradition variance worth noting for readers cross-referencing other printed editions.`,
    keyPoints: ["Vulgate mantra-ganana 1.54-55 = print true vv.1.42-43 at PDF pp.171-172, no speaker line, lineation preserved", "Source record in print: mula pp.171-172 with seven tikas (Jagaccandra 56-57; jagad-etac-caracaram patha noted)", "**Forceful Cosmic Delusion (*Balādākṛṣya*)**: Maya is a cosmic sovereign law, impossible to overcome by individual egoic will alone.","**Dual Function of Mahamaya**: She who binds the universe is the exact same Supreme Power who liberates."]
