    interpretiveNotes: [{ note: `Guptavati-adi sapta-tika edition (814-pp PDF, per source-provenance table): Adhyaya 1 at PDF pp.116-215. Mula of true vv.1.53-58 Brahma-stuti opening at PDF pp.182-192 (v.53 pp.182-183 Vishveshvari, v.54 pp.184-186 Svaha-Svadha, v.55 pp.187-189 Ardhamatra-Savitri, vv.56-57 p.190 Dharyate-Srijyate, v.58 p.192 Mahavidya). This digest entry highlights vv.55-58; PDF lineation for the highlighted padas is preserved above — brahmovaca speaker line into the hymn, two padas per verse.` }, { note: `Tika order on each verse 1.53-58: (1) Guptavati, (2) Chaturdhari, (3) Shantanavi (ardhamatra = avyakta turiya; yato vaco nivartante; gandhari-gandhara-svara), (4) Nagojibhatti, (5) Jagaccandrachandrika (mantra-counts 67-80 across this hymn opening), (6) Damshoddhara, (7) Chandraprabha Hindi. Verbatim record with rendering for the highlighted verses is given in the commentary below; the entry's highlight selection, translation and Sadhana-Samara alignment are untouched.` }],
    translation: `You are the eternal half-measure that cannot be uttered even by those who know its special nature; You alone are the Sāvitrī, and You, O Goddess, are the supreme Mother. By You this universe is held, by You this world is brought forth, by You, O Goddess, it is sustained, and always, at the end, You consume it. In emanation You are the form of creation, in maintenance the form of preservation, and likewise, at the end of this world, the form of dissolution, O You who pervade the universe. You are Mahāvidyā, Mahāmāyā, Mahāmedhā, Mahāsmṛti; You are Mahāmoha, and You are Mahādevī, the great demoness (to the demonic) and the great Goddess (to the divine).`,
    commentary: `Source structure, exactly as printed (Guptavati-adi sapta-tika, 814-pp PDF). Place in the edition: mula 1.53 at PDF pp.182-183 (vishveshvarim jagaddhatrim sthiti-samhara-karinim / nidram bhagavatim vishnor atulam tejasah prabhuh, then brahmovaca); mula 1.54 at pp.184-186 (tvam svaha tvam svadha tvam hi vashatkarah svaratmika / sudha tvam akshare nitye tridha matratmika sthita, with svaha-deva-havir-dane / matra tritiya cic-chaktir ardhamatra param padam nyayas quoted); mula 1.55 at pp.187-189 (ardhamatra-sthita nitya yanuccarya visheshatah / tvam eva sa tvam savitri tvam devi janani para, with tritiya tu plutardhakhya vacasah sa na gocara); mula 1.56-57 at p.190 (tvayaitad dharyate vishvam ... tvam atsyante ca sarvada / visrishtau srishti-rupa tvam ... jagato'sya jaganmaye); mula 1.58 at p.192 (mahavidya mahamaya mahamedha mahasmritih / mahamoha ca bhavati mahadevi mahasuri) — renderings as in translation below. Tikas in fixed seven order per verse. Diacritics are given here in simplified ASCII to keep the source record robust; the entry's highlight mula, translation and Sadhana-Samara plus Seven-Digest reading follow unaltered. **Sādhana-Samara Commentary:**
Having named Herself through the mantra-syllables in the previous verse, the Goddess now names Herself through cosmic *function*. This is the transition from Her identity as sound (śabda-tattva) to Her identity as world-process (jagat-tattva).
1. **The Unutterable Half-Measure (Ardhamātrā)**: Beyond the three audible mātrās of AUM (A-U-M) lies a fourth, silent half-measure — the *turīya*, the witness-state underlying waking, dream, and deep sleep. Devī is identified with precisely this silence beyond speech: the substratum that no mantra can fully capture, because it is what listens even to the mantra.
2. **The Threefold Verb (Dhāryate/Sṛjyate/Pālyate/Atsi)**: Four verbs — held, created, sustained, consumed — collapse the entire cosmic cycle into a single breath. This is deliberately economical: where earlier verses distributed creation-preservation-dissolution across Brahmā-Viṣṇu-Rudra, here all three collapse back into the single undivided Devī who was always their common ground.
3. **The Four Mahā-Names**: Mahāvidyā (great knowledge), Mahāmāyā (great illusion), Mahāmedhā (great intellect), Mahāsmṛti (great memory), and Mahāmoha (great delusion) are given in one breath without contradiction. The text refuses to let the sādhaka settle into an easy division between Devī's "good" liberating powers and Her "bad" binding ones — both are named Mahā (great), both are Her, and spiritual maturity is precisely the capacity to see this without flinching.

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
On verse 55, Santanavi is unusually expansive, working through several proposed derivations of "ardhamatra" - as the unvoiced half-beat completing the syllable Om, as the fourth, unspoken measure standing for the turiya state itself, and as "that which cannot be reached by words, which turn back from it together with the mind," explicitly invoking the Taittiriya Upanishad's language of the unreachable. Nagojibhatti's note here simply directs the reader back to its own remarks on the previous verse, treating 55 as a continuation of the same argument rather than a fresh topic - a sign the commentators read 54-55 as one philosophical unit. Guptavati keeps to the essentials: Savitri is glossed as the Gayatri mantra's presiding deity, "janani para" as the supreme mother precisely because, unlike ordinary mothers who beget only "this or that" limited offspring, she alone brings forth everything without exception. On verses 56-57, several commentators (Chaturdhari, Nagojibhatti) read the three verbs - dharyate, srjyate, palyate, atsi - as a direct restatement of Brahma-Vishnu-Rudra's three functions now reclaimed by the single Devi who underlies all three, with Santanavi spelling this out explicitly: creation by her Brahmi shakti, protection by her Vaishnavi shakti, dissolution by her Raudri shakti, three names for one undivided power. Verse 58's four "Maha-" names receive comparatively brief individual glosses (mostly grammatical formation notes) from Guptavati and Damsoddhara, with the commentators letting the verse's own accumulating rhythm - vidya, maya, medha, smrti, moha - carry its meaning without heavy interpretive overlay; the Hindi Candraprabha follows this same restrained approach, translating each name plainly in sequence.`,
