    translation: `You are Prakṛti itself, manifesting the three guṇas of everything; You are Kālarātri, Mahārātri, and dreadful Moharātri. You are Śrī, You are sovereignty itself, You are Hrī (modesty as power), You are intellect whose nature is understanding; You are shame, nourishment, contentment, peace, and endurance. Bearing a sword, a trident, terrible, bearing a mace, a discus, a conch, a bow, arrows, a spear, and an iron club as weapons; gentle, and more gentle than all gentle things, exceedingly beautiful beyond compare, You alone are the supreme among the high and low, the Supreme Sovereign. Whatever thing, existent or non-existent, exists anywhere, O all-pervading One, You are the power of all of that — so how, then, could You possibly be praised in full?`,
    commentary: `Source structure, exactly as printed (Guptavati-adi sapta-tika, 814-pp PDF). Place in the edition: mula 1.59 at p.193 (prakritis tvam ca sarvasya guna-traya-vibhavini / kalaratrir maharatrir moharatris ca daruna, with moharatrir mohatanur jagatsute fencing); mula 1.60 at p.194 (tvam shris tvam ishvari tvam hris tvam buddhir bodha-lakshana / lajja pushtis tatha tushtis tvam shantih kshantir eva ca); mula 1.61 at pp.195-196 (khadgini shulini ghora gadini cakrini tatha / shankhini capini bana-bhushundi-parighayudha, with bhujabhih shatrun mundyati iti bhushundi vyutpatti and dasha-bhuja Vaikritika quote); mula 1.62 at pp.197-198 (saumya saumyatara-shesha-saumyebhyas tvati-sundari / paraparanam parama tvam eva parameshvari); mula 1.63 at pp.198-199 (yac ca kincit kvacid vastu sad asad vakhilatmike / tasya sarvasya ya shaktih sa tvam kim stuyase tada, with maya iti kvacit-patha) — renderings as in translation below. Tikas in fixed seven order per verse (see notes above). Diacritics are given here in simplified ASCII to keep the source record robust; devanagari above preserves the print aksharas exactly, and the Sadhana-Samara plus Seven-Digest reading follows unaltered. **Sādhana-Samara Commentary:**
This is the great catalogue-hymn at the philosophical center of the *turīya*-praise: every register of existence — cosmic (Prakṛti), temporal (the three Rātris), ethical (Śrī, Hrī, Lajjā), martial (the nine weapons), aesthetic (saumyā, sundarī), and finally ontological (sat/asat, existent/non-existent) — is named as Her form, before the hymn admits its own impossibility.
1. **The Weapon-List as Interior Map**: Sword, trident, mace, discus, conch, bow, spear, club — nine implements corresponding, in the *sādhanā* reading, to nine faculties of discrimination the aspirant must develop: cutting through doubt (sword), piercing delusion (trident/śūla), and so on. The Goddess is not merely armed for external battle; every weapon is first forged in the practitioner's own inner life.
2. **The Turn to Impossibility**: The final couplet is the hymn's hinge and its humility: having just listed everything nameable, Brahmā concedes that *any* enumeration is structurally incomplete, since Devī is not one item among the list but the very power (*śakti*) by which the list itself, and everything in it — real or unreal — exists at all. This single verse is often cited as the *Devī Māhātmya*'s clearest statement that Devī-praise can never be exhaustive, only gestural.

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
Pradipa gives its fullest attention in this stretch to the weapon-catalogue of verse 61, tracing each named implement to the specific deity said elsewhere in the text to have gifted it - tying this verse forward to the weapon-donation scene of chapter 2 - and reading the accumulation as proof that Devi's martial capacity was never borrowed for a single occasion but is intrinsic to her nature from the very moment of her praise in chapter 1, well before Mahisha's war even begins. Nagojibhatti and Damsoddhara divide their notes mainly between resolving compound formations (guna-traya-vibhavini, sad-asad-vakhilatmike) and confirming variant readings found across manuscript traditions. Santanavi reads the qualities of verse 60 - Sri, Hri, buddhi, lajja, pusti, tusti, shanti, kshanti - as together constituting a complete inventory of what classical thought calls the sattvika endowments of a well-formed mind, so that the verse quietly instructs the sadhaka in what inner qualities to cultivate even while ostensibly describing the Goddess. On the closing verse's rhetorical question "how then are you to be praised," Guptavati and the Hindi Candraprabha agree this is not despair but the hymn's deliberate self-undercutting - a device meant to humble the one praising rather than to diminish the object of praise, consistent with apophatic gestures found elsewhere in Puranic stotra literature.`,
